# Monero Inflation Checker

This work, "MIC - Monero Inflation Checker", is a derivative of:

"Mininero" by ShenNoether (https://github.com/monero-project/mininero) and
"dumb25519" by SarangNoether (https://github.com/SarangNoether/skunkworks/tree/curves/dumb25519)

the FCMP++ part incorporates [monero-oxide](https://github.com/monero-oxide/monero-oxide) by kayabaNerve, licensed under the MIT License.

"MIC - Monero Inflation Checker" is licensed under GPL 3.0 by DangerousFreedom.

## TLDR

Minimal Python tools and educational material for checking inflation in Monero,
plus a separate tool for FCMP++ on testnet. You can get more information at
[moneroinflation.com](https://www.moneroinflation.com/).

## What is it?

**MIC** (`MIC.py`) is the original checker, against **mainnet**. It verifies real
transactions as they appear on chain: v1 ring signatures, MLSAG, CLSAG, and
Borromean / Bulletproof / Bulletproof+ range proofs, plus the commitment balance
that pins the amounts. It also sums the emission curve. 

**The FCMP++ tool** (`fcmp_tool.py`) is a **separate tool, for testnet**. 
It generates and verifies complete FCMP++ transactions in pure Python with
C++ bindings using the OpenSSL library for the Helios/Selene operations. 

The tools here are just for educational purposes. They are not ready for
production.

## Installation and requirements

Python >= 3.10. Install into a fresh virtual environment:

```bash
python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate

pip install -r requirements.txt   # pinned versions, reproducible
# or
pip install -e .                  # editable install, looser ranges
```

To install the constant-time Helios/Selene bindings:

```bash
pip install pybind11                                          # needs g++ (C++17)
bash mic/fcmp/bindings/build.sh                               # and OpenSSL headers
python -c "from mic.fcmp.bindings import ct; print(ct.backend())"   # which path is live
```

See [`mic/fcmp/bindings/README.md`](mic/fcmp/bindings/README.md).

### Node access

MIC talks to a mainnet node, either your own full node
(`http://localhost:18081`) or a public one (Cake Wallet's, by default). It asks
which on startup. To change the node to connect to, modify this file:
[`mic/common/settings_df25519.py`](mic/common/settings_df25519.py).

The FCMP++ tool takes `--node URL` and defaults to `http://localhost:28081`.
Access is read-only and works against a restricted RPC endpoint.

## Running MIC (mainnet)

```bash
python3 MIC.py            # interactive menu 
```

The menu offers: verify one transaction by id, verify a single block, scan the
chain from where it left off, and scan every point and scalar in it. Progress is
kept in `stats/` so a scan can be resumed.

## Running the FCMP++ tool (testnet)

```bash
python fcmp_tool.py --help
python fcmp_tool.py verify-tx --help
python fcmp_tool.py scan --help
```

## Tests

FCMP++: 

```bash
python -m unittest mic.tests.fcmp.test_end_to_end  # prove, verify, assemble a tx, replay a scan
python -m unittest mic.tests.fcmp.test_units       # every unit test, one section per topic
```

The end to end test can be run directly to pick the tree depths it builds:

```bash
python -m mic.tests.fcmp.test_end_to_end --layers 4          # one depth
python -m mic.tests.fcmp.test_end_to_end --layers 1,3,6,8    # the default
MIC_FCMP_LAYERS=4 python -m unittest mic.tests.fcmp.test_end_to_end
```

Each depth is deterministic, so it always produces the same transaction down to
the byte, and each is written out to `proofs/fcmp_proof_<n>layers.json` in the
shape a daemon returns a transaction in: a finished FCMP++ proof as it would sit
in a block, together with the curve tree root it opens.

Legacy:

```bash
python -m unittest mic.tests.legacy.test_units         # df25519, CLSAG, range proofs
python -m unittest mic.tests.legacy.test_checkers      # the v1 inflation check
python -m unittest mic.tests.legacy.test_create_proof  # BP+ prove/verify round trip
python -m unittest mic.tests.legacy.test_verify_types  # one real tx per signature type
```

